Using the Effective Field Theory of Large Scale Structure, we make predictions for HI intensity mapping power spectrum multipoles based on perturbation theory. These predictions will allow us to constrain cosmological parameters using mildly non-linear scales from data that will be obtained from interferometric HI intensity mapping experiments. Assuming that the experiments will be like CHORD and PUMA, we run a series of analyses on mock data that accurately represents how these surveys are conducted at redshift z=0.5, comparing these to the performance that can be expected from stage 4 optical galaxy surveys. The impact of removing foregrounds using methods that rely on simulations is accounted for in the analysis, which shows how it affects the accuracy of the parameter estimation. The accuracy of the parameter estimation depends on the model used to fit the data, with higher accuracy achieved when using an EFT-based model than when a local model is used. On the other hand, removing foregrounds significantly degrades the accuracy unless very high precision is achieved. The accuracy achieved on a range of parameters including the primordial power spectrum, the matter density and the algebraic coefficient by several models including a version of MICE that takes into account neutrino mass is also presented. The implications of these findings on the design of intensity mapping experiments are discussed, which suggests the need for higher frequency observations than previously considered to control for foregrounds properly. Furthermore, we examine the potential of these experiments to pin down HI bias, discovering that both the algebraic and mangager coefficients can be well constrained if the data goes up to k~0.3 h/Mpc. In a future paper, we will present the prediction for the EFT model parameters, which includes the damping scale, while also exploring the potential of these experiments to discover new physics in the EFT framework. The code to reproduce these results